  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 306 AM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nAn upper trough will continue its trek into the northern Rockies on \nFriday, with flow aloft becoming increasingly westerly over New \nMexico. The change in the flow pattern will favor east/west facing \nslopes instead of north/south ones, but very saturated soils from \nrecent rainfall will still support isolated instances of flash \nflooding. Drying will occur from west to east as the sub-tropical \nplume gets disrupted by the passage of the trough axis. While \nSaturday will be drier, the increased forcing associated with the \ntrough axis should increase shear and support a few strong to severe \nstorms. \n\nMoisture is finally scoured out on Sunday, but an uptick is likely \nagain early next workweek as the subtropical jet stream becomes more \nactive. Both the GEFS and EPS ensemble mean PWATs are showing \nmoisture 150-200% of normal over New Mexico by the middle of next \nweek, with moderate to high confidence that this elevated moisture \nwill linger into the last week of the month.",
